    <DataNode elementId="AT1G31860-MONOMER" textLabel="AT1G31860-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O82768" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The Arabidopsis genome has a single copy gene that encodes a bifunctional enzyme catalyzing the second and the third step of histidine biosynthesis. Complementation experiments with the N- and C-terminal domains separately demonstrated that they are responsible for the phosphoribosyl-AMP cyclohydrolase and phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphohydrolase activities, respectively. The protein sequence has an N-terminal chloroplast transit signal peptide.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G31860-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G31860.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O82768" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-13373, RXN-13374" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G31860.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G31860" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_9733547" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT4G26900-MONOMER" textLabel="imidazoleglycerol phosphate synthase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SZ30" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The conversion of BBM III to imidazole glycerol phosphate and AICAR in histidine biosynthesis requires two catalytic functions, including a transfer of an amide from glutamine followed by a cyclization reaction. In prokaryotes, these two catalytic activities are encoded by two genes whose proteins form a complex. In yeast and plants, this conversion is catalyzed by a bifunctional enzyme. Like yeast, the N-terminal and the C-terminal domains of the Arabidopsis enzyme are responsible for the glutamine amidotransferase and the cyclase activities, respectively. The Arabidopsis protein sequence has a chloroplast transit signal peptide. The enzyme is encoded by a single copy gene in Arabidopsis.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G26900-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT4G26900.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SZ30" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="imidazoleglycerol-phosphate synthase" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-13378" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT4G26900.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT4G26900" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_9654139" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT4G39120-MONOMER" textLabel="AT4G39120-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q6NPM8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">HISN7/IMPL2 encodes a mulitfunctional enzyme that is involved in histidine biosynthesis. Its ability to dephosphorylate histidinol-phosphaste was demonstrated through functional complementation of a &lt;i&gt;Streptomyces coelicolor hisN&lt;/i&gt; mutant |CITS: [20023146]|. A conserved glycine residue, Gly195, is required for this activity |CITS: [20023146]|. In addition, loss of this gene caused embryonic lethality in homozygous mutants that could be partially overcome through exogenous supplementation of histidine indicating that no other genes are functionally redundant with HISN7/IMPL2, at least in the developing embryo |CITS: [20023146]|. Recombinant HISN7/IMPL2 protein has also been shown to accept L-galactose 1-phosphate, D-myo-inositol 3-phosphate and D-myo-inositol 1-phosphate as good substrates for desphosphorylation |CITS: [19339506]|. However, an &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; role for HISN7/IMPL2 in myo-inositol and ascorbate metabolism has not yet been demonstrated |CITS: [20023146]|. Expression studies show that this gene is expressed throughout the plant across multiple developmental stages. The lowest level of expression is reported in the pollen. A similar expression pattern has been observed for other genes in the histidine biosynthetic pathway |CITS: [20023146]|. In addition, like other participants in the pathway, this enzyme is localized in the chloroplast |CITS: [20023146]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G39120-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT4G39120.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q6NPM8" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Panther" value="PTHR20854" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Smr" value="Q6NPM8" />
